      "category": "boundary condition",
      "severity": "medium",
      "summary": "Changing `>=` to `>` breaks the documented boundary: orders of exactly 100 now receive no discount.",
      "explanation": "The function's docstring at line 5 explicitly states \"Orders of 100 or more get 10% off.\" The new comparison `if order_total > 100:` excludes order totals equal to 100, so `calculate_discount(100.0)` now returns `0.0` instead of `10.0`, contradicting the documented contract (inclusive lower bound). This is an off-by-one boundary regression on the threshold value.",
      "evidence": "Line 5 docstring: \"Return the discount amount. Orders of 100 or more get 10% off.\" Line 6: `if order_total > 100:` — the `>=` was replaced by `>`, making 100 exclusive.",
      "suggested_fix": "Restore the inclusive comparison: `if order_total >= 100:` so that an order total of exactly 100 still qualifies for the 10% discount described in the docstring.",
